<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>GSTories - Long Reads</title><style>body{margin:0;font-family:system-ui;background:#0b0f19;color:#e2e8f0}header{padding:16px;background:#111827;position:sticky;top:0;display:flex;justify-content:space-between;align-items:center;border-bottom:1px solid #1f2937}a{color:#38bdf8;text-decoration:none}.wrap{max-width:800px;margin:0 auto;padding:16px}.card{background:#121a2b;border:1px solid #1e293b;border-radius:16px;padding:18px;margin:12px 0;cursor:pointer}.card:hover{border-color:#38bdf8}.tag{display:inline-block;font-size:11px;padding:3px 8px;border-radius:999px;background:#1e293b;border:1px solid #334155;margin-right:6px}#reader{display:none;position:fixed;inset:0;background:#020617;overflow:auto;padding:20px;z-index:50}.cover{width:100%;max-height:380px;object-fit:cover;border-radius:16px;margin:14px 0;border:1px solid #1e293b}h1{line-height:1.2}pre{white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:inherit;line-height:1.8;font-size:16px}</style></head><body><header><b style="color:#fff">GSTories.com</b><a href="index.html">Home</a></header><div class="wrap"><h2 style="color:#fff">Nigerian Long True Life Stories</h2><p>Tap any story to read - 10 to 15 mins each.</p><div id="list"></div></div><div id="reader"><div class="wrap"><a href="#" onclick="closeR();return false">← Back to stories</a><img id="rImg" class="cover" style="display:none"><h1 id="rTitle" style="color:#fff"></h1><div id="rMeta"></div><pre id="rBody"></pre><a href="#" onclick="closeR();return false">← Back</a></div></div><script>
const stories=[
{t:"Under The Khaki Shadow - Nigeria 1994 [TRUE LIFE - FEATURED]",d:"A family in Onitsha under military regime. How radio changed everything.",mins:"12 mins",img:"IMG-20261008-WA0000.jpg",body:`COVER IMAGE: The old radio on the table.

CHAPTER 1: THE ANNOUNCEMENT
It was August 1994. I was 8 years old. My mother was frying akara in front of our house in Awada, Onitsha. NEPA had taken light for 3 days. The small battery radio was our only connection to the world.

Suddenly, the radio presenter shouted: "Fellow Nigerians, the military government has announced..."

Everybody stopped. My mother left the akara to burn. Our neighbor, Papa Emeka, ran out with his wrapper tied half.

The announcement said there would be curfew from 7pm to 6am. No one should be outside. Soldiers would shoot on sight.

That night, we did not sleep. We heard boots. Gbakam! Gbakam! Soldiers marching through our street.

CHAPTER 2: THE NIGHT SOLDIERS CAME
By 8pm, Onitsha was dead silent. Even dogs were afraid to bark.

My father had not returned from Main Market. My mother was crying inside, praying in Igbo: "Chukwu biko..."

At 9:15pm, we heard a loud bang on our gate. "OPEN THIS GATE!"

It was two soldiers with torch and rifle. My mother thought they came for my father.

The younger soldier said, "Mama, we saw light from your window. Curfew is on."

My mother begged. She offered them the remaining akara and cold garri.

The older soldier looked at me - small boy peeping from curtain - and said, "Put off that lantern. Next time, we will not knock."

They left. But that night I learned what fear under khaki truly means. It is not the gun. It is the power to decide if you live or sleep.

CHAPTER 3: THE RADIO MAN
Next morning, Papa Emeka brought his big radio to our compound. He became the news man for our street.

Everybody gathered around him at 6am before going to market. He would translate the news from English to Igbo.

"They say Abiola still in prison," he would say. "They say petrol will be 11 naira now."

One day, the soldiers seized his radio at Upper Iweka because he was playing it loud. He cried like a child. That radio cost his 3 months savings.

We contributed money in our street - 20 naira, 50 naira - and bought him a smaller one from Nkpor.

That is how we lived. One radio, one street, one fear.

CHAPTER 4: MY FATHER'S RETURN
My father returned after 2 days. He had slept inside his shop at Main Market because soldiers blocked Onitsha Bridge.

He said soldiers flogged traders who came late. He saw a man forced to frog-jump from Main Market to Upper Iweka carrying a bag of rice on his head.

My father brought home nothing that day. No sale. But he brought home a story that made my mother say, "God, when will this military end?"

That question stayed in our house for 5 years. Until 1999.

CHAPTER 5: LESSON
Today when I hear young people complain about Nigeria, I remember 1994.

We had no phone. No internet. No light. Only radio and fear.

But we also had community. One radio for 10 houses. One pot of soup for 3 families when market was blocked.

The khaki shadow taught us resilience. It taught us that a people can survive anything if they hold each other.

If you lived through 1993-1999, share your story in comments. Let the young ones know where we are coming from.

THE END.`},
{t:"The Girl at Nkpor Junction",d:"She waved okada every day, but never entered.",mins:"3 mins",body:"She waved okada every day, but never entered. One day I stopped. She said 'I'm not waiting for bike, I'm waiting for my brother who never came back from travel since 2019.' I gave her my phone to call, she smiled and left. Some people are not lost, they are waiting."},
{t:"My Hostel Night Horror",d:"Room 12 in Chukwuemeka Odumegwu hostel.",mins:"4 mins",body:"Room 12 in my hostel, everyone says someone died there. I didn't believe until that night rain was falling and I heard someone dragging chair. I opened eyes, my roommate was sleeping, but chair was moving alone. I shouted Jesus! NEPA took light, everywhere dark. I ran outside."},
{t:"When My Mother Sold Her Wrapper",d:"To pay my JAMB form in 2021.",mins:"3 mins",body:"My mother sold her favorite wrapper to pay my JAMB form in 2021. I failed JAMB that year. I cried because I wasted her wrapper. She said 'wrapper can be bought, your education cannot.' I read again and passed. That wrapper is now framed in our house."},
{t:"The Keke Man Who Returned My Phone",d:"I forgot my phone in keke at Upper Iweka.",mins:"2 mins",body:"I forgot my phone in keke at Upper Iweka. I thought it's gone. At 10pm, someone called my sister's phone: 'Your brother forgot phone, come to 3-3.' He waited 2 hours. Honest people still exist in Onitsha."}
];
const list=document.getElementById('list');
stories.forEach((s,i)=>{
const d=document.createElement('div');d.className='card';
d.innerHTML=`<div><span class="tag">${s.mins}</span>${s.img?'<span class="tag" style="color:#38bdf8">📷 PHOTO STORY</span>':''}</div><h3 style="margin:8px 0 6px;color:#fff">${s.t}</h3><p style="margin:0;opacity:.8">${s.d}</p>`;
d.onclick=()=>openR(i);list.appendChild(d);
});
function openR(i){
const s=stories[i];document.getElementById('rTitle').innerText=s.t;
document.getElementById('rMeta').innerHTML=`<span class="tag">${s.mins}</span><span class="tag">${s.d}</span>`;
document.getElementById('rBody').innerText=s.body;
const im=document.getElementById('rImg');
if(s.img){im.src=s.img;im.style.display='block';im.onerror=()=>{im.src='IMG-20261008-WA_.jpg';}}else{im.style.display='none';}
document.getElementById('reader').style.display='block';window.scrollTo(0,0);
}
function closeR(){document.getElementById('reader').style.display='none';}
</script></body></html>
